= Probability distribution =

== Overview ==
A '''probability distribution''' describes how probability is allocated across the possible values of a random variable.

Distributions can be discrete, continuous, mixed, univariate, or multivariate.

== Discrete distributions ==
For a discrete random variable X, a probability mass function assigns:

<pre>
p(x) = P(X = x)
</pre>

with:

<pre>
p(x) ≥ 0
Σ p(x) = 1
</pre>

Examples include Bernoulli, binomial, and Poisson distributions.

== Continuous distributions ==
For a continuous random variable, a probability density function f(x) can satisfy:

<pre>
P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = ∫[a,b] f(x) dx
</pre>

A density value is not itself the probability that X equals exactly that point.

== Cumulative distribution ==
The cumulative distribution function is:

<pre>
F(x) = P(X ≤ x)
</pre>

Every real-valued random variable has a CDF, including discrete and continuous cases.

== Distribution and data ==
A theoretical distribution is a model.

An observed histogram or empirical distribution summarizes data.

Fitting a named distribution does not prove that it is the true data-generating process.

== Parameters ==
Distribution families can have parameters controlling location, scale, shape, rate, or dependence.

Statistical inference estimates or reasons about these parameters from data.

== Related concepts ==
[[Probability]], [[Conditional probability]], and [[Bayesian inference]] connect distributions to the probability calculus and statistical updating.

